{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Keeping kindness</b> — the father for the sons, if they are good." "<b>Bearing iniquity.</b> Set your mind to my words. Behold it is written וחזקת והיית לאיש (1 Kings 2:2), and he was already a man; rather its meaning is a man of valor and renown. And behold it is written כי זקנתי מהיות לאיש (Ruth 1:12), and the meaning is: from being in the world, or a woman to a man, for she was female. And behold ואבואה אליה (Genesis 29:21) is the lying down, for she was female. And behold אשר באו אליך הלילה (ibid. 19:5) is not from this meaning, for it is a male. And so too החוטא ונשא עונו (Leviticus 5:1) — the sin is not atoned for him; rather it is the punishment that comes upon him, in the manner of ולצת לבדך תשא (Proverbs 9:12), and ועתה שא נא חטאתי (above 10:17), for Moses was righteous. And the principle is: one who bears his iniquity upon himself is always to his disgrace. And bearing of iniquity is upon two paths: the first path, that He forgives him — this is praise for the bearer of iniquity. And the other path, that the bearer of iniquity is a wicked man — then for the bearer of iniquity it is disgrace, and for the one who bears the iniquity [i.e., the forgiver] it is praise — like ונשא את עונה (Numbers 30:16), for the punishment is removed from her and is upon him." "And bearing <b>transgression.</b> Know that transgression is more severe than iniquity, as though the transgressor goes out from under the authority, like אז תפשע אדום מתחת יד יהודה (2 Kings 8:22). And behold iniquity is less than it, and it is with intent." "And the word חטאה is like חטאת, and it is a noun, and it is not like הממלכה החטאה (Amos 9:8), for that is an adjective. And the word חטאה includes every thought and every inadvertence in deed, for iniquity is in deed or in speech. And behold we examined the prayer of Moses concerning the matter of the spies, and he said YHWH, which is the proper name, and the attributes are predicated upon it, and he did not mention the second name, which is an epithet — for the Name wanted to destroy — and not <b>El.</b> For there is no need to mention this attribute. And not <b>compassionate.</b> For Israel were not simpletons. And not <b>gracious.</b> For no violence was done to them that they should cry out to HaShem to deliver them. And he mentioned <b>slow to anger.</b> For they were wicked. <b>And abundant kindness.</b> — to all it is. And he did not mention ואמת, for they were liable. And he mentioned <b>bearing iniquity and transgression.</b> And he did not mention וחטאה, for they were not inadvertent. And from the great humility that was in Moses, he included himself with Israel, to say וסלחת לעונינו ולחטאתינו, and he did not mention פשעינו on account of his great stature, for it is not fitting that he should transgress."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {3 "Where Ibn Ezra parses why Moses omitted certain divine attributes in his prayer after the spies, I have left his compressed logical connectors (כי, ולא) untranslated where they chain together without finite verbs, preserving his rapid-fire clause structure."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:40:24.332469559Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}